// #3242. The inlined back-edge poll guard
// (compiler/arm64call.bit's emitInlinedPollGuard / compiler/x64call.bit's
// xEmitInlinedPollGuard, both mirroring runtime/stw/stwpoll.bit's
// stwPollNeeded) has TWO independent clauses:
//
//   1. atomicLoad(world + worldStopWord) != 0          -- a stop is pending
//   2. a collection is due -- heap pressure. Since #3359 that clause mirrors
//      the runtime predicate in full: the byte trigger
//      `atomicLoad(heap + heapLiveWord) >= gcNextBytes` AND then either the
//      second growth cycle or `gcNumObjectsIdx >= gcNextObjects`. It only
//      ever fires LESS often than the byte trigger alone did, so everything
//      this gate argues about clause 2 not masking clause 1 still holds a
//      fortiori.
//
// _tests_/bit/pollguardoffsets.bit used to pin the word offsets these clauses
// read against the runtime's real exported consts — but that gate compared two
// LITERALS and never ran a single compiled program, so it could not tell
// whether the COMPILED poll actually parks a mutator. #3240 found exactly that
// gap: compiler/codegen.bit's pgWorldStopWordWord() was 1280 for the whole time
// muWords was 6 (worldStopWord moved to 1536 in #2646), pointing every emitted
// poll at slot 213's `muAcked` — a word that is always 0, structurally (slot
// 213 needs 213+ concurrently claimed mutators, never reached) — which
// permanently disarmed clause 1. The suite stayed green, including
// `BIT_GC=stress` at 65/65, because clause 2 independently parks any allocating
// mutator and every corpus program allocates.
//
// #4203 RETIRED THAT GATE, and this one is what survives. No backend mirrors a
// runtime word offset any more: the back edge reads one word the runtime
// publishes into (runtime/sched/pollreq.bit), and only running a compiled
// program can show that the publishing side and the reading side agree.
//
// THIS GATE closes that: it drives clause 1 IN ISOLATION, with clause 2
// structurally unable to fire, and asserts the COMPILED poll actually parks
// an allocation-free mutator.
//
// WHY NOT A SPAWNED-THREAD-PLUS-USER-SPACE-LOCK APPROACH. #3240's engineer
// tried exactly that and reported it inconclusive, for three specific
// reasons this harness avoids by construction rather than by care:
//
//   - `spawn`'s process-exit semantics wait for every spawned green thread
//     rather than being fire-and-forget -> the mutator loop below is bounded
//     (Power of 10, rule 2) AND short-circuited by `testDone`, so it always
//     returns and `wg.wait()` below always unblocks, win or lose.
//   - OS-worker-thread-to-slot mapping is not stable/observable from user
//     code -> this harness never needs to know the mutator's own slot.
//     `worldRendezvous` scans every slot but the caller's own and reports a
//     single pass/fail; which slot the spawned task lands on is irrelevant.
//   - allocating inside a held world lock self-deadlocks -> nothing between
//     `worldLockAcquire` and `worldLockRelease` below prints, allocates, or
//     calls anything but the four `@nosplit` runtime doors used here, none
//     of which reach `gcAlloc`.
//
// THE CLAUSE THIS KILLS: clause 1, `atomicLoad(world + worldStopWord) != 0`.
// Clause 2 cannot mask it here because the mutator loop never calls anything
// that reaches `gcAlloc` — heapLiveWord is therefore pinned at whatever this
// program's own tiny FIXED setup left it at (a WaitGroup's channel, three
// one-word slices — all allocated before the timed section, and orders of
// magnitude under `gcDefaultMinTrigger`, 4MiB) for the whole window between
// `worldLockAcquire` and `worldRestart`.
//
// MUTATION-PROVEN TWICE. #3242: reverting compiler/codegen.bit's
// pgWorldStopWordWord() to 1280 and rebuilding made this gate FAIL —
// "worldStopWord clause did not park", exit 1, abandoned after ~6ms. #4203:
// making runtime/sched/pollreq.bit's `pollRequest` store 0 instead of 1 makes
// it FAIL the same way, exit 1, abandoned after 181ms. Both outputs are on
// their tickets.
//
// REQUIRES BIT_WORKERS >= 2 (set in this gate's own tools/build/gatestable2.bit
// entry, not left to the caller's environment). Boot's scheduler default is
// ONE OS worker thread (runtime/root/rootconfig.bit's `BIT_WORKERS`, default
// 1 via `rootEnvWorkers(1)`) — measured directly: under that default this
// harness does not fail fast, it runs for 100+ seconds, because the single
// worker never comes back to the controller task while the allocation-free
// loop below is running (a plain arithmetic loop has no cooperative yield
// point; only `spawn`/channel ops/`sleep`/a blocking syscall give the
// scheduler a chance to switch tasks). The iteration ceiling two billion
// lines down is sized to fail that misconfiguration fast (a few seconds)
// rather than reading as a hang, not to bound the intended (multi-worker)
// run, which never gets near it.
//
// #4030: `stopped` ABOVE CANNOT DISTINGUISH "polls only when told" FROM
// "polls on every iteration". Reproduced directly: inverting this file's own
// clause 1 sense in compiler/arm64call.bit (`emitLocalCbz(cx, true, rWorld)`
// -> `emitLocalCbz(cx, false, rWorld)` for the "stop pending" test) still
// PASSES this file unmodified — `stopped` still comes back true, elapsedMs
// still ~0ms. The reason: the inverted clause makes the guard call the real
// `bit_rt_safepoint` on essentially every iteration whenever the stop word
// reads 0 (the overwhelming majority of the run), rather than only when it
// is nonzero. `stwPoll` inside that call is untouched by the mutation and
// still makes the correct park decision every time it is reached — it is
// simply reached constantly instead of rarely, so a call already in flight
// (or the very next one) always straddles the controller's brief stop
// window. `checkThroughput` below closes this: it cannot be fooled by an
// inversion that preserves "parks eventually", because #2921/#2925 exist
// specifically because calling bit_rt_safepoint on every back edge is
// expensive (an unconditional call cost ~58% of collatz's runtime) — an
// always-poll guard collapses this loop's own iteration rate, which
// `stopped` never observes but a rate comparison does.
//
// WHY A RATIO AGAINST A `@nosplit` REFERENCE LOOP, NOT AN ABSOLUTE COUNT.
// Tried first: assert `mutatorLoop`'s own iteration count (during the
// settle window it already runs) is above a fixed floor. Both a single
// later-spawned-probe version AND this settle-window version were
// eventually broken by real fleet contention on this box (#4030): readings
// that were 35-55M at load 39 fell to 7.3M at a self-induced load in the
// 60s, well under any fixed floor wide enough to also pass at load 39.
// Wall-clock throughput is not load-invariant, so no single absolute
// threshold survives an unbounded range of contention.
//
// A RATIO is, because `refLoop` below is `@nosplit` — §10.3.1 makes the
// compiler skip poll-guard emission at its back edge ENTIRELY, for any
// build under test here including a mutated one (`needsNoSafepoints(cx.f)`
// in `emitBackEdgeSafepointIfNeeded` is checked before
// `emitInlinedPollGuard` is ever called), so nothing this file mutates can
// touch `refLoop`'s rate. Whatever ambient contention does to one
// allocation-free loop on this box it does to both roughly in proportion,
// so `mutatorLoop`'s rate divided by `refLoop`'s stays close to constant
// across load while their INDIVIDUAL rates both swing by 10x+.
//
// #4310: the ORIGINAL version of this check used `sleep(20 * Millisecond)` as
// the window and judged ratio_x1000 against a floor of 150. That sleep never
// delivered anything near 20ms — with the @nosplit refLoop occupying a
// worker and nothing else idle, no worker takes a turn to expire the timer
// until a PARKED worker hits idleParkMaxNs (100ms), so the real window was
// 100-102ms in every direct measurement taken (45/45 macOS, 8/8
// x86_64-linux). Past `preemptBudgetNs` (10ms, runtime/sched/preempt.bit)
// `sysmonTick` flags the @nosplit refLoop's worker for preemption, which it
// can never clear (no safepoint reachable in an @nosplit body), so
// `preemptAnyPending()` latches true and EVERY back edge legitimately takes
// the slow path for the remainder of the run — the ratio was measuring an
// unmeasured, uncontrolled window well past its own knee, which is why two
// honest measurement sessions on the SAME unmodified tree recorded
// non-overlapping bands (100-141 one session, 145-172 another): neither
// figure was wrong, both were snapshots of one broad, scheduler-dependent
// distribution with no floor that could separate it from the mutant
// reliably.
//
// checkThroughput below instead busy-waits an EXACT 5ms on
// bit_rt_time_mono_ns (well under preemptBudgetNs, so refLoop's worker is
// never flagged) and asserts the window it actually measured rather than
// the one it asked for. ratio_x1000 = mutatorLoop_iters * 1000 /
// refLoop_iters, both sampled at the same instant right after the window
// closes. MEASURED with this instrument (#4258, re-confirmed #4310) at 2ms,
// 5ms and 10ms windows, idle box through deliberately induced load
// (aarch64-macos ~18 and ~33-50, x86_64-linux quiet):
//
//   aarch64-macos correct           832-910
//   aarch64-macos always-poll mutant  0-30
//   x86_64-linux correct            750-795
//   x86_64-linux always-poll mutant  20-24
//
// Floor 400: >1.9x margin under the correct band's floor and >16x over the
// mutant's ceiling, on both platforms, and neither band moves materially
// between load ~18 and load ~50.

import { exit } from "std/os"
import { newWaitGroup, WaitGroup } from "std/sync"
import { monotonic, since, sleep, Millisecond, Second } from "std/time"
import {
  worldBlockAddr, currentMutator, worldLockAcquire, worldLockRelease,
  worldRendezvous, worldRestart, wordAt,
} from "../../runtime/gc"

// #4310: the throughput window's own clock. Reached as a runtime-archive
// symbol rather than through std/time's `monotonic()`, which returns a
// heap-allocated `Mono` (#2678) unusable inside a tight measurement loop.
// Same spelling `runtime/sched/idle.bit` uses for the identical reason.
extern fn bit_rt_time_mono_ns(): int

class Shared { wg: WaitGroup, started: []i64, testDone: []i64, iters: []i64 }

// The mutator under test: allocation-free, with a real loop back edge so the
// compiler emits the back-edge poll guard at it. See the header for why
// heapLiveWord cannot move while this runs.
//
// #4030: publishes its own progress every 2^14 iterations into `s.iters`
// (not every iteration — that would add its own overhead to the very rate
// `main`'s throughput check below is trying to measure) so the controller
// can read, at the end of the settle sleep it already does, how far this
// SAME already-running loop got, for comparison against `refLoop` below.
// #4310: was 2^20 — over a properly-measured 5ms window (see the file
// header) that published only 2-4 quanta, making the ratio a near-binary
// signal instead of a rate. 2^14 gives ~150 quanta over the same window.
fn mutatorLoop(s: Shared) {
  atomicStore(ptrOf(s.started), 1)
  let i = 0
  // Bounded per Power of 10 (rule 2). Two billion is a hard ceiling that is
  // never actually reached in a correctly configured (BIT_WORKERS >= 2) run,
  // win or lose — the controller always raises `testDone` within
  // milliseconds. It exists to fail fast under BIT_WORKERS=1 instead of
  // hanging (see header): measured at ~485M iterations/s, so this ceiling
  // resolves in a few seconds rather than 100+.
  while (i < 2000000000 && atomicLoad(ptrOf(s.testDone)) == 0) {
    i = i + 1
    if ((i & 16383) == 0) {
      atomicStore(ptrOf(s.iters), i)
    }
  }
  s.wg.done()
}

// #4030: the reference loop the throughput ratio is measured against — see
// the file header for why `@nosplit` (never gets a poll guard emitted at its
// back edge, §10.3.1, regardless of any mutation to emitInlinedPollGuard) is
// what makes the ratio load-insensitive. Same publish shape as mutatorLoop.
@nosplit fn refLoop(testDone: []i64, iters: []i64) {
  let i: i64 = 0
  while (i < 2000000000 && atomicLoad(ptrOf(testDone)) == 0) {
    i = i + 1
    if ((i & 16383) == 0) {
      atomicStore(ptrOf(iters), i)
    }
  }
  atomicStore(ptrOf(iters), i)
}

// `wg.done()` is not itself `@nosplit` (WaitGroup uses a channel), so it
// cannot be called from inside refLoop's body (E0075) — this wrapper is the
// ordinary, non-nosplit task spawn calls instead.
fn refLoopTask(wg: WaitGroup, testDone: []i64, iters: []i64) {
  refLoop(testDone, iters)
  wg.done()
}

// #4030: throughput ratio check — see the file header for the full
// rationale and the measurements behind 400. Runs as its OWN self-contained
// phase, spawning and fully joining a private pair of loops BEFORE `main`
// spawns the stop-word test's own mutatorLoop below.
//
// MUST NOT RUN CONCURRENTLY WITH THE STOP-WORD TEST — found directly (#4030)
// by trying it: `refLoop` never polls at all (that is the whole point of
// making it `@nosplit`), so a mutator slot running it can never report
// itself stopped. `worldRendezvous` below requires EVERY slot but the
// caller's own to report stopped (`runtime/gc/gcworldstop.bit`'s own scan),
// so an alive, never-parking `refLoop` mutator makes `worldRendezvous`
// abandon every single time regardless of whether the guard under test is
// correct — reproduced on an UNMUTATED tree: `test-pollguardstopword` failed
// 4/4 with "worldStopWord clause did not park" once refLoop ran alongside
// it. Spawning this phase first and fully joining it (`wg.wait()` below)
// before phase two's mutatorLoop is spawned removes the extra mutator slot
// entirely by the time worldRendezvous scans, while still giving both loops
// here the same favourable "freshly spawned" scheduling the measurements in
// the header depend on — as the very first spawns in the process, not spawns
// competing with an already-running mutator.
fn checkThroughput() {
  let started = []i64(1)
  let testDone = []i64(1)
  let iters = []i64(1)
  let refIters = []i64(1)
  let wg = newWaitGroup()
  wg.add(2)
  spawn mutatorLoop(Shared{ wg: wg, started: started, testDone: testDone, iters: iters })
  spawn refLoopTask(wg, testDone, refIters)

  // #4310: an EXACT busy-wait on the monotonic clock, not sleep() -- see the
  // file header for why sleep(20 * Millisecond) never delivered anything
  // near 20ms. 5ms is well under preemptBudgetNs (10ms), so sysmonTick has
  // not yet flagged refLoop's @nosplit worker for preemption.
  let wStart = bit_rt_time_mono_ns()
  let deadline = wStart + 5000000
  while (bit_rt_time_mono_ns() < deadline) {}
  let realNs = bit_rt_time_mono_ns() - wStart

  let mutIters = atomicLoad(ptrOf(iters))
  let refItersAt = atomicLoad(ptrOf(refIters))

  atomicStore(ptrOf(testDone), 1)
  wg.wait()

  // #4310: judge the window actually measured, not the one asked for. Past
  // preemptBudgetNs, sysmonTick flags refLoop's worker and it can never
  // clear the flag (no safepoint reachable in an @nosplit body), so
  // preemptAnyPending() latches true and every back edge legitimately takes
  // the slow path for the rest of the run -- a window that long is not a
  // measurement of clause 1 in isolation.
  if (realNs > 8000000) {
    println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — throughput window measured ${realNs}ns, want <= 8000000ns; a window this long crosses preemptBudgetNs and stops being a measurement of clause 1 alone (mut=${mutIters} ref=${refItersAt})")
    exit(1)
  }

  // #4310: refLoop may not have run its first publish yet at very short
  // windows -- observed once directly. A zero denominator is a harness
  // defect, not a throughput result.
  if (refItersAt == 0) {
    println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — reference loop published 0 iterations over a measured ${realNs}ns window; it was not dispatched in time to measure against (mut=${mutIters})")
    exit(1)
  }

  let ratioX1000 = mutIters * 1000 / refItersAt
  if (ratioX1000 < 400) {
    println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — allocation-free mutator reached ${mutIters} iterations against the nosplit reference loop's ${refItersAt} over a measured ${realNs}ns window with nothing pending (ratio_x1000=${ratioX1000}, want >= 400); the back-edge poll guard is calling bit_rt_safepoint far more often than the runtime's published state justifies, masking a broken clause behind an always-poll fallback")
    exit(1)
  }

  println("pollguardstopword: throughput PASS — mut=${mutIters} ref=${refItersAt} ratio_x1000=${ratioX1000} window=${realNs}ns")
}

fn main() {
  // #4030: a SEPARATE, independent, and separately-phased check -- see
  // checkThroughput's own comment for why it must run to completion before
  // this phase's mutatorLoop spawns, and the file header for why `stopped`
  // below cannot catch a clause that polls unconditionally instead of only
  // when told.
  checkThroughput()

  let started = []i64(1)
  let testDone = []i64(1)
  let iters = []i64(1)
  let wg = newWaitGroup()
  wg.add(1)
  spawn mutatorLoop(Shared{ wg: wg, started: started, testDone: testDone, iters: iters })

  // Bounded wait for the mutator to publish that it is inside its loop.
  // Racing an as-yet-unscheduled task would let `worldRendezvous` pass
  // trivially against an idle, gcBlockingBegin/End-bracketed worker and
  // prove nothing.
  let startDeadline = monotonic().ns + 5 * Second
  while (atomicLoad(ptrOf(started)) == 0) {
    if (monotonic().ns > startDeadline) {
      println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — mutator loop never started")
      exit(1)
    }
    sleep(1 * Millisecond)
  }
  sleep(20 * Millisecond) // settle solidly inside the loop, not its first iteration

  let waddr = worldBlockAddr()
  if (waddr == 0) {
    println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — worldBlockAddr() returned 0, runtime not ready")
    exit(1)
  }
  let selfMu = currentMutator(0)
  if (selfMu == 0) {
    println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — controller could not claim a mutator slot")
    exit(1)
  }
  let w = wordAt(waddr)

  // `monotonic()` now returns a `Mono` (#2678) — a heap-allocated class — so
  // both readings are taken outside the locked section; `t0` and `elapsed`
  // are diagnostic only (`stopped` is what drives pass/fail below), so
  // bracketing the lock instead of being taken exactly at its edges costs
  // nothing but the two @nosplit calls' own overhead.
  //
  // Nothing between acquire and release may print, allocate, or call
  // anything but these three @nosplit doors (see header, self-deadlock note).
  let t0 = monotonic()
  worldLockAcquire(w)
  let stopped = worldRendezvous(w, selfMu - 1)
  worldRestart(w)
  worldLockRelease(w)
  let elapsedMs = since(t0) / Millisecond

  atomicStore(ptrOf(testDone), 1)
  wg.wait()

  if (!stopped) {
    println("pollguardstopword: FAIL — the stop request did not park the allocation-free mutator; worldRendezvous abandoned after ${elapsedMs}ms (worldRendezvous is not calling pollRequest, or the compiled back edge is not reading runtime/sched/pollreq.bit's attention word)")
    exit(1)
  }

  println("pollguardstopword: PASS — allocation-free mutator observed and parked by the worldStopWord clause (${elapsedMs}ms)")
}
